  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/">Guides</a></li>
      <li aria-current="page">Soil, Pots &amp; Drainage</li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h1>Soil, pots &amp; drainage</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    Roots need air as well as water. Soil that stays wet suffocates them, and
    that is the root cause — literally — of most overwatering damage. Good
    drainage is not a nice extra; it is the foundation everything else is
    built on.
  </p>

  <h2>Drainage holes are not optional</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Every pot a houseplant lives in should have at least one drainage hole,
    with a saucer underneath to catch the runoff. A layer of pebbles at the
    bottom of a pot without holes does not fix the problem — water still
    pools at the bottom, out of reach of the roots until it is too late.
    If you love a decorative pot with no holes, use it as a cover pot: keep
    the plant in a plastic nursery pot with holes inside it, and tip out any
    water that collects after watering.
  </p>

  <h2>Matching soil to the plant</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Most houseplants do well in a general-purpose potting mix with some
    perlite or bark mixed in for air. Succulents and cacti need a much
    grittier mix — roughly half potting mix, half perlite or coarse sand —
    so water runs through quickly. Ferns and calathea, by contrast, like a
    mix that holds a little more moisture. One bag of soil does not suit
    every plant.
  </p>

  <h2>When to repot</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Repot when roots are circling the bottom of the pot, water runs straight
    through without wetting the soil, or growth has stalled in the growing
    season despite good light and feeding. Move up only one pot size — a pot
    much larger than the root ball holds wet soil the roots cannot reach,
    which invites rot.
  </p>

